{"maxId":274,"entries":{"chapter-53-study-hip-fractures|Chapter 53 Study: Hip Fractures":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-epidemiology|Definition & epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|Osteoporosis and propensity to fall are prominent risks. Average age is 82; approximately 18% of women and 36% of men die within the first postfracture year. (p. 793)":["u4"],"diagnosis-amp-repair|Diagnosis & repair":["u5"],"copy|Painful, shortened, externally rotated leg is typical; nondisplaced fractures may still permit walking. Persistent suspicion with negative radiographs → MRI, more sensitive than CT. (p. 794)":["u6"],"copy|Displaced femoral-neck fractures threaten the head's blood supply and are usually treated with arthroplasty; intertrochanteric fractures have better blood supply and generally heal with internal fixation. (p. 794)":["u7"],"management-amp-recovery|Management & recovery":["u8"],"copy|Reconcile medications and individualize surgical decisions using prior function, cognition, comorbidities and goals. Nonsurgical care may suit selected patients with very high operative risk or very short life expectancy. (p. 797)":["u9"],"copy|Mobilize on the day of surgery if possible, certainly by postoperative day 1; remove an inserted catheter the following morning. Monitor delirium, pulmonary complications and heart failure. (p. 797)":["u10"],"copy|Match rehabilitation to functional loss, endurance, ability to participate and home support. US Medicare inpatient rehabilitation generally requires 3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week, or 15 hours within 7 consecutive days in documented cases. At least two therapy disciplines are needed, including PT or OT (p800; CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria).":["u11"],"copy|Hazzard gives 21–31 days of postoperative thromboprophylaxis; NICE recommends one month after fragility hip/proximal femur fracture when VTE risk outweighs bleeding risk. Up to 50% of previously independent older adults may need walking aids at one year (p801; NICE NG89, recommendation 1.11.2).":["u12"],"drugs-amp-prevention|Drugs & prevention":["u13"],"copy|Individualize analgesia for sustained relief and therapy-related pain while minimizing sedation; the optimal regimen is not established here. A preoperative nerve block can reduce pain. (p. 797)":["u14"],"copy|Replete vitamin D before starting a bisphosphonate; check the level during admission if not measured recently. (p. 798)":["u15"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u16"],"copy|Walking does not exclude a nondisplaced fracture. (p. 794)":["u17"],"copy|General versus spinal anesthesia: no mortality difference was found. (p. 797)":["u18"],"copy|Slow recovery warrants assessment for persistent delirium, depression, medication burden, nutritional deficiencies and isolation. (p. 800)":["u19"],"recall|Recall":["u20"],"past-paper-questions-on-this-chapter|Past-paper questions on this chapter":["u21"],"figure-53-5-book-image|Figure 53-5 — book image":["u27"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 53-5, p.800":["u28","u48"],"copy|CHAPTER 53s / HAZZARD":["u29"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 53; the book is the reference.":["u30"],"copy|Full chapter available: Read Chapter 53.":["u31"],"copy|Fast build from the book; not lane-reviewed.":["u32"],"copy|1.":["u33"],"copy|Which two underlying risks are emphasized for hip fracture?":["u34"],"copy|2.":["u35"],"copy|How do the approximate first-year mortality estimates differ by sex?":["u36"],"copy|7.":["u37"],"copy|Is nonsurgical care excluded for every hip fracture?":["u38"],"copy|9.":["u39"],"copy|When should mobilization and catheter removal occur?":["u40"],"copy|12.":["u41"],"copy|What nonmedical factors can determine safe discharge home?":["u42"],"copy|13.":["u43"],"copy|What participation requirements distinguish acute inpatient rehabilitation?":["u44"],"copy|14.":["u45"],"copy|What should be investigated when recovery is slower than expected?":["u46"],"copy|Answer":["u49","u51","u57","u59","u63","u65","u69"],"copy|Osteoporosis and propensity to fall. (p. 793)":["u50"],"copy|Approximately":["u52"],"copy|18%":["u53"],"copy|of women and":["u54"],"copy|36%":["u55"],"copy|of men are expected to die during the first year. (p. 793)":["u56"],"copy|No. It may be considered for selected patients, including those with very high surgical risk, very short life expectancy, or advanced dementia with contractures; prior function and goals matter. (p. 797)":["u58"],"copy|Mobilize on the day of surgery if possible, certainly by postoperative day":["u60"],"copy|1":["u61"],"copy|; remove an inserted catheter the morning after surgery. (p. 797)":["u62"],"copy|Home layout, social support, caregiver availability and resources, alongside mobility and self-care ability; a home assessment by therapy staff supplies essential information. (p. 798)":["u64"],"copy|In a US Medicare inpatient rehabilitation facility, generally":["u66"],"copy|3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week":["u67"],"copy|, or":["u67"],"copy|15 hours in 7 consecutive days":["u67"],"copy|in documented cases. Multiple therapy disciplines are required, including PT or OT (p800; CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria).":["u68"],"copy|Persistent delirium, depression, polypharmacy, vitamin D or other nutritional deficiencies, and social isolation. (p. 800)":["u70"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Chapter 53, printed pages 793–804.":["u32"],"copy|Match rehabilitation to functional loss, endurance, ability to participate and home support. Hazzard requires the ability to participate in at least 3 hours of therapy per day and input from at least two rehabilitation disciplines, such as PT and OT (p800).Current practice (not the book): CMS generally requires intensive therapy 3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week or, in documented cases, 15 hours within 7 consecutive days, with multiple disciplines including PT or OT. CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria.":["u11"],"copy|Hazzard gives 21–31 days of postoperative thromboprophylaxis. Up to 50% of previously independent older adults may need walking aids at one year (p801).Current practice (not the book): NICE recommends one month after fragility hip/proximal femur fracture when VTE risk outweighs bleeding risk. NICE NG89, recommendation 1.11.2.":["u12"],"copy|A subtrochanteric break lies within 5 cm below the lesser trochanter, and the displaced fragments in 2020 Q57 require a nail spanning the break (p794 and the 2017 hip-fracture review, Figures 2–3 and pp.":["u106"],"copy|Hazzard says total replacement is thought to give better long-term results and less pain for active patients (p794).":["u199"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): In the later HEALTH trial of previously ambulatory patients with displaced neck fractures, the average functional advantage at two years was clinically small.":["u112"],"copy|Consider the person's usual activity and preferences when discussing this trade-off.":["u114"],"copy|Hazzard found no mortality difference between spinal and general anesthesia and describes peripheral nerve block for pain.":["u200"],"copy|The printed exam locator p869 concerns dementia; hip-fracture anesthesia is on p797.":["u137"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Spinal anesthesia did not improve survival or walking recovery in REGAIN and delirium rates were similar.":["u134"],"copy|ASRA bases neuraxial timing on apixaban dose and elapsed time; one fixed two-day interval does not cover every patient.":["u201"],"copy|REGAIN · ASRA fifth edition, apixaban recommendations.":["u138"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): NICE advises one month of prophylaxis after fragility hip/proximal femur fracture when thrombotic risk outweighs bleeding risk.":["u202"],"copy|Hazzard requires the ability to participate in":["u203"],"copy|at least 3 hours of therapy per day":["u204"],"copy|and input from":["u205"],"copy|at least two rehabilitation disciplines":["u66"],"copy|, such as PT and OT (p800).":["u206"],"copy|CHAPTER 53 / HAZZARD":["u29"],"copy|June 2023 Q47 — mcq-78b3359272a717f584c23b2c":["u22"],"copy|May 2024 Q11 — mcq-a6c338a57828b765465f7cbf":["u23"],"copy|May 2024 Q17 — mcq-72e468cf141dda15964157c9":["u24"],"copy|May 2024 Q35 — mcq-d59bcda0cc381a35cc3134db":["u25"],"copy|June 2025 Q60 — mcq-10163f2b99f5abe875d7d917":["u26"],"copy|May 2024 Q17":["u47"],"hip-fractures|Hip Fractures":["u207"],"fracture-patterns-and-repair|Fracture patterns and repair":["u208"],"operative-timing-and-team-care|Operative timing and team care":["u209"],"recovery-and-complications|Recovery and complications":["u210"],"what-recovery-looks-like-over-a-year|What recovery looks like over a year":["u81"],"prevent-complications-through-the-handoff|Prevent complications through the handoff":["u82"],"rehabilitation-and-discharge|Rehabilitation and discharge":["u211"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u212"],"copy|Up to 50% of previously independent older adults may need walking aids at one year (p801).":["u213"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u214"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u215"],"copy|Read the fracture pattern before choosing repair.":["u216"],"copy|Timing, medical stability and early mobility then shape recovery and discharge.":["u217"],"copy|Is the native head present?":["u93"],"copy|A postoperative film may show a prosthetic head and stem.":["u218"],"copy|In a displaced intracapsular fracture, the fine retinacular vessels along the neck are vulnerable.":["u95"],"copy|Even fragments held in good alignment can go on to nonunion or osteonecrosis.":["u96"],"copy|Arthroplasty avoids relying on the head's viability.":["u97"],"copy|A nondisplaced or impacted neck fracture retains alignment and may be stabilized in situ.":["u98"],"copy|A person who previously walked with a frame still has mobility to preserve (pp.":["u99"],"copy|794–795, Figure 53-2).":["u100"],"copy|Painful, shortened, externally rotated leg is typical; nondisplaced fractures may still permit walking.":["u219"],"copy|Persistent suspicion with negative radiographs → MRI, more sensitive than CT. (p.":["u220"],"copy|794)":["u221","u225","u232"],"copy|Follow the screw.":["u101"],"copy|A screw that passes through the neck into the native head and joins a short nail in the proximal femur.":["u222"],"copy|This construct points to an intertrochanteric fracture.":["u103"],"copy|The trochanteric region has enough collateral circulation to heal once stable.":["u104"],"copy|A stable pattern can receive a sliding hip screw.":["u105"],"copy|A subtrochanteric break lies within 5 cm below the lesser trochanter, and displaced subtrochanteric fragments require a nail spanning the break (p794 and the 2017 hip-fracture review, Figures 2–3 and pp.":["u223"],"copy|2059–2060).":["u107"],"copy|Displaced femoral-neck fractures threaten the head's blood supply and are usually treated with arthroplasty; intertrochanteric fractures have better blood supply and generally heal with internal fixation. (p.":["u224"],"copy|Total versus hemiarthroplasty.":["u108"],"copy|Both replace the femoral head.":["u109"],"copy|A total replacement also resurfaces the socket.":["u110"],"copy|Current practice: Total versus hemiarthroplasty.":["u226"],"copy|In the later HEALTH trial of previously ambulatory patients with displaced neck fractures, the average functional advantage at two years was clinically small.":["u227"],"copy|Secondary procedures occurred with similar frequency, and instability or dislocation was more common after total replacement.":["u113"],"copy|HEALTH trial, 2019.":["u115"],"copy|The man who used a walker before fracturing his hip can still benefit from repair and early mobilization.":["u116"],"copy|The article tested in 2020 and 2022 advised surgery within 48 hours when medically feasible.":["u117"],"copy|Surgery within 24 hours of admission was associated with lower mortality in observational cohorts.":["u118"],"copy|People ill enough to require stabilization also waited longer, so the association does not give an individual causal estimate.":["u119"],"copy|Prolonged bed rest carries risks even when surgery is inappropriate.":["u120"],"copy|Management of Acute Hip Fracture, pp.":["u121"],"copy|2057, 2059–2060.":["u122"],"copy|Start geriatric assessment on admission.":["u123"],"copy|Confirm baseline walking and cognition with someone who knows the patient, reconcile medicines and identify active conditions that can be stabilized promptly.":["u124"],"copy|In pooled studies, shared orthogeriatric care was associated with lower in-hospital and long-term mortality and shorter stays.":["u125"],"copy|In one randomized ward trial, mobility at four months improved without a reduction in in-hospital delirium.":["u126"],"copy|Recovery of walking, delirium and survival are distinct outcomes (pp.":["u127"],"copy|795–797).":["u128"],"copy|Apixaban and the anesthetic plan.":["u129"],"copy|Current practice: Neuraxial anesthesia after apixaban.":["u228"],"copy|Spinal anesthesia did not improve survival or walking recovery in REGAIN and delirium rates were similar.":["u229"],"copy|Pitfall.":["u230","u253"],"copy|Walking does not exclude a nondisplaced fracture. (p.":["u231"],"copy|When a previously independent patient asks whether he will walk as before, separate survival from specific tasks.":["u139"],"copy|The mortality estimates are highest in those with poor health and particularly during the early months.":["u140"],"copy|Of those who survive, many regain meaningful mobility but need a cane, assistance on stairs or help with shopping.":["u141"],"copy|In the cohort shown on p799, some people who once dressed independently still needed help putting on trousers one year later (pp.":["u142"],"copy|793–794, 799, 801).":["u143"],"copy|Figure 53-5 times the best observed recovery in each domain.":["u144"],"copy|Mood, cognition and upper-extremity activities generally reach their peak near four months.":["u145"],"copy|Balance and gait reach a plateau near nine months.":["u146"],"copy|Lower-extremity activities and instrumental tasks can continue to improve to about a year.":["u147"],"copy|These are typical trajectories.":["u148"],"copy|They do not imply that a patient with ongoing early limitation will remain at that level (p800).":["u149"],"copy|Match rehabilitation to functional loss, endurance, ability to participate and home support.":["u233"],"copy|Hazzard requires the ability to participate in at least 3 hours of therapy per day and input from at least two rehabilitation disciplines, such as PT and OT (p800).":["u234"],"copy|Current practice: Inpatient therapy intensity.":["u235"],"copy|CMS generally requires intensive therapy 3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week or, in documented cases, 15 hours within 7 consecutive days, with multiple disciplines including PT or OT.":["u236"],"copy|CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria.":["u237"],"copy|Lower-limb function may improve over a year; depression can improve within about half a year.":["u238"],"copy|The figure places the typical depression plateau near four months.":["u151"],"copy|The preceding page describes symptom reports through six months.":["u152"],"copy|Even transient depressive symptoms can accompany poorer functional recovery.":["u153"],"copy|Ask about mood, delirium, medications, poor intake and isolation when function stalls (pp.":["u154"],"copy|799–801).":["u155"],"copy|Count from the operation.":["u156"],"copy|For thromboprophylaxis, distinguish fragility fracture from elective arthroplasty.":["u239"],"copy|A patient making short assisted walks may remain immobile most of the day.":["u158"],"copy|Document the prophylactic agent, planned stop date and bleeding assessment at transfer.":["u159"],"copy|The chapter specifies 21–31 days after surgery (p801).":["u160"],"copy|Reassess skin, bowel function, pain, weight, cognition and other active illnesses in the rehabilitation setting.":["u162"],"copy|Stage II or worse pressure injuries developed in about 5% within 21 days in the report cited by the chapter (p801).":["u163"],"copy|Current practice: Thromboprophylaxis duration.":["u240"],"copy|